import{_ as a,o,c as t,a3 as n}from"./chunks/framework.DhF4LrUq.js";const u=JSON.parse(`{"title":"FAQ","description":"Frequently asked questions about ShipperDog, answered from the system's real behavior - invitations, installs, approvals, promotion blockers, CI tokens, and what a 404 really means.","frontmatter":{"description":"Frequently asked questions about ShipperDog, answered from the system's real behavior - invitations, installs, approvals, promotion blockers, CI tokens, and what a 404 really means."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/faq.md","filePath":"reference/faq.md"}`),r={name:"reference/faq.md"};function s(i,e,h,l,d,p){return o(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="faq" tabindex="-1">FAQ <a class="header-anchor" href="#faq" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FAQ&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The questions people actually ask, answered from how the system really behaves. Persona quickstarts: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/roles/">all roles</a>. Deeper mechanics: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/reference/troubleshooting">troubleshooting</a>.</p><h2 id="testers" tabindex="-1">Testers <a class="header-anchor" href="#testers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Testers&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="my-invitation-link-does-not-work-now-what" tabindex="-1">My invitation link does not work. Now what? <a class="header-anchor" href="#my-invitation-link-does-not-work-now-what" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;My invitation link does not work. Now what?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Invitation links are <strong>single-use and expire after seven days</strong>. If the page tells you to open the link from your email, the link was already used or has gone stale — ask your team for a fresh invitation and use the new link. Nothing about your account is lost; activating through the new link is the same one-tap flow: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/portal/accept-invite">accept an invitation</a>.</p><h3 id="why-can-t-i-see-an-application-on-my-home-screen" tabindex="-1">Why can&#39;t I see an application on my home screen? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-can-t-i-see-an-application-on-my-home-screen" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why can&#39;t I see an application on my home screen?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The portal lists exactly what you hold <strong>grants</strong> for: an active membership in the tenant, plus a grant for the application (explicit per version, by application, by tag, or &quot;latest N&quot;). If a colleague sees an app you do not, your grant or membership is the difference — ask your QA or admin team; there is no setting on your side. See <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/concepts/authorization">how access is decided</a>.</p><h3 id="i-tapped-download-but-the-link-expired" tabindex="-1">I tapped Download but the link expired. <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-tapped-download-but-the-link-expired" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I tapped Download but the link expired.&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Download links are <strong>short-lived by design — minutes</strong>. That is the signed-URL model: no storage locations, no long-lived tokens. Tap <strong>Download</strong> again on the version page and a fresh link is minted for you. If the portal instead says the build is not available, the team pulled it or your access changed — that one is not a link problem (<a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/portal/install-ios">iOS</a> / <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/portal/install-android">Android</a>).</p><h3 id="my-iphone-refuses-the-install" tabindex="-1">My iPhone refuses the install. <a class="header-anchor" href="#my-iphone-refuses-the-install" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;My iPhone refuses the install.&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Direct iOS installs work <strong>only for builds your team prepared for registered devices</strong>. A refused install means the build was not prepared for your device — ask your team; they can send the same build through TestFlight instead, which has no per-device preparation. Details: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/portal/install-ios">install on iOS</a>.</p><h3 id="my-android-download-finished-but-nothing-installed" tabindex="-1">My Android download finished but nothing installed. <a class="header-anchor" href="#my-android-download-finished-but-nothing-installed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;My Android download finished but nothing installed.&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Android requires a one-time permission before a browser may install apps: <strong>Settings - Apps - your browser - Install unknown apps</strong>, allow from this source. You do this once per browser; then open the downloaded file and confirm. Step-by-step: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/portal/install-android">install on Android</a>.</p><h2 id="release-managers" tabindex="-1">Release managers <a class="header-anchor" href="#release-managers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Release managers&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="who-approves-my-release" tabindex="-1">Who approves my release? <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-approves-my-release" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who approves my release?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The <strong>approval workflow attached to the ring</strong> decides — a named chain of roles (ALL/ANY/conditional semantics) defined by your organization. The production ring always requires an approval policy; anything past a policy hold or a blocking gate stops there. The release dashboard names the workflow and shows the round&#39;s standing: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/console/approvals">work with approvals</a>.</p><h3 id="can-i-approve-my-own-release" tabindex="-1">Can I approve my own release? <a class="header-anchor" href="#can-i-approve-my-own-release" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can I approve my own release?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>No. The creator of a release can never approve it, <strong>whatever roles they hold</strong> — the delegation rule (<code>APPROVAL_SELF_APPROVAL</code>). Ask a holder of the required role; the error names it when you try.</p><h3 id="why-is-my-promotion-blocked" tabindex="-1">Why is my promotion blocked? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-is-my-promotion-blocked" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why is my promotion blocked?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Promotion evaluates gates, approvals, tenant eligibility, and release policies before committing the hop, and a refusal lists <strong>every</strong> blocker with evidence — not just the first. Run the precheck (it executes the same evaluation as a read), fix what it names, and promote again. Every refusal is recorded on the promotion timeline: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/console/rings-and-promotion">promote through rings</a>.</p><h3 id="what-does-no-data-yet-fail-closed-on-a-gate-mean" tabindex="-1">What does &quot;no data yet (fail-closed)&quot; on a gate mean? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-does-no-data-yet-fail-closed-on-a-gate-mean" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What does &quot;no data yet (fail-closed)&quot; on a gate mean?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Required gates <strong>block when they have no result</strong> — missing data is a refusal, never a pass. Metric-backed gates resolve from analytics; the others need a result with an evidence URL from your pipeline. If the data genuinely cannot arrive in time, a release manager or organization admin can override once, with a mandatory, audited reason: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/console/quality-gates">work with quality gates</a>.</p><h3 id="a-release-went-out-bad-what-happens-when-i-roll-back" tabindex="-1">A release went out bad. What happens when I roll back? <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-release-went-out-bad-what-happens-when-i-roll-back" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A release went out bad. What happens when I roll back?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The ROLLED_BACK crossing <strong>cuts downloads at the moment of the crossing</strong>: a tester outside the window gets the same &quot;no version available&quot; answer as one never granted — never a stale binary. The reason you give rides the state timeline permanently; nothing is deleted. Testers who already installed keep the installed app — rollback stops distribution, it does not reach onto handsets: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/console/rollback">roll back a release</a>.</p><h2 id="developers-and-ci" tabindex="-1">Developers and CI <a class="header-anchor" href="#developers-and-ci" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Developers and CI&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="my-ci-upload-exited-with-code-4-what-broke" tabindex="-1">My CI upload exited with code 4. What broke? <a class="header-anchor" href="#my-ci-upload-exited-with-code-4-what-broke" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;My CI upload exited with code 4. What broke?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Exit 4 means the API rejected the request — the printed problem names the exact cause: wrong file type for the platform (<code>ARTIFACT_FILE_TYPE_REJECTED</code>), a build number already used (<code>ARTIFACT_BUILD_TAKEN</code>), a frozen version (<code>VERSION_IMMUTABLE</code>), or an archived application. Each has a one-line fix in <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/reference/error-codes#cli-and-ci">error codes</a>.</p><h3 id="the-pipeline-suddenly-exits-3-—-is-that-my-token" tabindex="-1">The pipeline suddenly exits 3 — is that my token? <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pipeline-suddenly-exits-3-—-is-that-my-token" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The pipeline suddenly exits 3 — is that my token?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Exit 3 is an authentication failure: no stored credentials or a 401. Tokens do not silently last forever — mint a fresh personal access token, and prefer <code>SHIPPERDOG_TOKEN</code> in CI so nothing is written to disk. See <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/automation/cli-reference#before-the-first-command">the CLI reference</a>.</p><h3 id="what-is-the-difference-between-a-version-and-an-artifact" tabindex="-1">What is the difference between a version and an artifact? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-the-difference-between-a-version-and-an-artifact" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is the difference between a version and an artifact?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The <strong>version</strong> is the logical release-train (7.12.0); the <strong>artifact</strong> is the concrete immutable binary per platform (iOS build 421, Android build 842). A version carries one artifact per platform, and its artifact set freezes the moment any release references it — new binaries mean a new version: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/get-started/concepts">core concepts</a>.</p><h2 id="everyone" tabindex="-1">Everyone <a class="header-anchor" href="#everyone" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Everyone&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="i-get-a-404-for-something-i-know-exists-bug" tabindex="-1">I get a 404 for something I know exists. Bug? <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-get-a-404-for-something-i-know-exists-bug" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I get a 404 for something I know exists. Bug?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Almost never. Authorization denials are <strong>indistinguishable from absence</strong> — a caller without access receives the byte-identical 404 a genuine miss produces, so the response never leaks what exists. Check your membership/grant/role first; administrators can see the exact denial in the audit trail. Full reasoning: <a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/reference/troubleshooting#a-404-for-something-you-know-exists">troubleshooting</a>.</p><h3 id="where-do-i-see-exactly-what-shipped-in-a-release" tabindex="-1">Where do I see exactly what shipped in a release? <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-do-i-see-exactly-what-shipped-in-a-release" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where do I see exactly what shipped in a release?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The <strong>release manifest</strong> — frozen and immutable at approval — records what shipped, its provenance, and its targets. Open it on the release dashboard or <code>shipperdog release manifest &lt;id&gt;</code> (<a href="/shipperdog/snapshot/how-to/automation/cli-reference">CLI reference</a>).</p>',36)])])}const m=a(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
